recommended.locality_source:
The engine's own declaration, read fresh on every publish on the asset-plane address the Editor is about to publish to. It is never configured, never inferred from the address and never probed.

Wire: a new closed request {"locality":{}} (empty struct, deny_unknown_fields). It joins parse_request's exactly-one set, which becomes scene | fetch | package | locality; the refusal text at Shared/asset-plane/src/publish.rs:476-479 and 506-511 changes to match. The reply is LocalityReply, tagged `reply` like PublishReply (publish.rs:36-50):
- Declared{locality}, which serialises as {"reply":"declared","locality":"CoLocated"|"Lan"}, using Locality's serde form (Shared/contracts/src/lib.rs:244-250);
- Refused(PublishRefusal), so an engine built before 5.10 answers with its existing InvalidPublishRequest and the Editor reports that as a named PublishRefused, not as Protocol.

Server: a new required method AssetPublishPeer::locality(&self) -> Locality, with no default, so no peer can declare by omission (publish.rs:80-109). Implementations:
- the worker Engine returns self.locality, the field capability() reads (engine.rs:74, 885);
- MockEngine returns self.locality (lib.rs:45, 377);
- conformance RecordingPeer delegates to its engine (conformance/src/editor_commands.rs:103);
- the two asset-plane test peers (handoff.rs:332, publish.rs:527) declare a field.

Client: a new public gx_asset_plane::declared_locality(engine: SocketAddr) -> Result<LocalityReply, PublishClientError>, built on the existing bounded exchange() (publish.rs:317-341). It is called only inside gx_asset_plane::publish_as_package, before anything is written, so Editor code never holds or passes a Locality. Errors map as follows:
- Connect error: EditorError::Unreachable. The existing unreachable test still holds (Editor/shell/tests/publish.rs:119-138).
- Malformed reply: Protocol.
- Refused: PublishRefused.

Integrity: conformance compares declared_locality(asset address) with EngineCapability.locality read over the control plane on:
- the real worker started with --package-dir;
- the real worker started with --lan;
- MockEngine::new() and MockEngine::new().over_lan(), each behind the control transport with an AssetPublishServer bound on server.engine(), as mocks/mock-engine/src/main.rs:101 does, extending run_over_transport at conformance/src/main.rs:163-215.

Doc comments that name the capability exchange as the source are amended to say: the declaration is made once, in the engine's single locality field; it is carried in EngineCapability and also answered on the asset plane for publishers that hold no control client. The files are Shared/control-plane/src/capability.rs:1-6 and Shared/asset-plane/src/handoff.rs:6-8.

recommended.done_when_text:
The Editor's publish runs cmd_publish → gx_editor::publish::publish_document → gx_asset_plane::publish_as_package. It learns the engine's declared locality only from the engine itself: a closed {locality:{}} read on the asset-plane address it publishes to, answered from the same field the engine declares as EngineCapability.locality. The Editor holds no control-plane client (R6) and no Editor code names a Locality (R9).

The publish always writes one verified package into the Editor's package folder: GX_EDITOR_PACKAGE_DIR if set, else platform::package_exchange_root(). tools/launch-shells.mjs and gx-engine-host give a co-located engine that same folder as --package-dir. The package is named by content hash, pid and sequence, never by user text. It goes through gx_asset_plane::publish_package: by path with 0 asset bytes when co-located, chunked with only the missing bytes over a LAN. It is removed after the reply. A LAN-declared engine reads no package path: one rule in the shared asset-plane server, and gx-render-worker exits 2 on --lan with --package-dir. Nothing about the package directory goes on the wire or into EngineCapability.

Executed by `cargo run -p gx-conformance`, peer section 'the Editor's publish against real render engines, co-located and LAN (5.10)', against two real gx-render-worker processes (one with --package-dir D, one with --lan) and MockEngine both ways:

(1) For every peer, gx_asset_plane::declared_locality(asset address) equals EngineCapability.locality read over that peer's control client: (CoLocated, Lan) for the workers, and each mock as started.

(2) Publishing: gpu-lower-third-50.json, with no revision, is opened in a gx_editor Workspace, and Inter-Regular.ttf is promoted into an AssetStore. package_sources and publish_document then publish it with folder D, using only each worker's asset address:
- the co-located worker reports LocalPath, 0 asset bytes, and a package inside D;
- the LAN worker reports ChunkedUpload with exactly 310252 bytes;
- both are Published with one content hash, equal to the document's own content_hash();
- D holds no .gpxpkg afterwards.

(3) Taking: each reply's (take_id, revision, content_hash) is taken over that worker's control client and is Taken on both. Below T0 that is a named TierTooLow SKIP, never a PASS. The row is done only when both are Taken on the T0 workstation.

(4) Each of the following is refused by name and reaches the shell as PublishRefused, never Unreachable:
- an Editor folder other than D: PackagePathOutsideDirectory naming D;
- a co-located worker started without --package-dir: PackageDirectoryNotConfigured;
- a by-path offer to a LAN-declared in-process Engine and a MockEngine::new().over_lan().with_package_directory(d), each holding a directory: PackageDirectoryNotConfigured.

(5) Also:
- a scene asset with no content hash refuses PackageAssetNotPackaged before any socket opens, and no file is written;
- a stopped engine is reported Unreachable;
- `gx-render-worker --lan --package-dir D` exits 2 naming the conflict;
- a co-located re-publish issues revision 2 with the same hash.

Mutations, each of which must FAIL a check above and is then restored byte-identical:
- publish_as_package passes a constant locality instead of the declared one;
- the worker's locality() answers the other variant;
- the mock's locality() always answers CoLocated;
- the server's LAN rule is removed;
- the package removal is skipped.

Also:
- the 5.8 and 5.9 suites still pass unchanged;
- `cargo test -p gx-asset-plane`, `cargo test -p gx-editor` and `cargo test -p gx-editor-shell --test publish` pass, the last with no SKIP on the workstation;
- `npm run check` exits 0, and check:boundaries shows no Editor-side manifest naming gx-control-plane, gx-control-transport or gx-playout;
- live, through tools/launch-shells.mjs, the running Editor's Publish button logs a by-path publication with 0 bytes, and Playout takes that revision.

